From: Any OS
Subject: [PATCH] d3d11: track IMMUTABLE resources that are created empty

Problem: DXMT marks every IMMUTABLE buffer and texture allocation GpuReadonly,
which is the same flag bit as NoTracking: ArgumentEncodingContext::access()
records no reader or writer for it, so no encoder ever waits on a fence for
it.  That is sound while the contents arrive with the create call (the
initializer queue, whose event every chunk waits for, or a CPU store).  The
native embedder cannot do that: the Neptune guest driver creates an IMMUTABLE
resource empty and sends the initial data as a RESOURCE_UPDATE, which the
host replays as ID3D11DeviceContext::UpdateSubresource (virglrenderer
src/neptune/npt_resource.c; npt_contents.c restores snapshots the same way).
The upload is then a blit encoder on the command stream, and the blit or draw
that reads the resource next has no fence to wait for.  The resources are
hazard-untracked in Metal, so the GPU may run the reader first and it sees
the zero-initialized allocation.

Windows 11 hits this at every composition atlas: dcomp writes the opaque
white texel at (0,0) of an application's shared BGRA surface atlas with
CopySubresourceRegion from a 1x1 IMMUTABLE texture, right after that texture's
upload (blit encoders N and N+1 of one command buffer, no fence between them).
When the copy won, the texel stayed 00000000 for the life of the atlas.  DWM
draws every solid-colour mask primitive of the atlas as
t0.Sample(uv0 = (0,0)) * colour * mask.a, so all of them vanished: taskbar
clock, input indicator, network and volume glyphs, weather text, Settings
sidebar text (issue #1107).  Which atlases lost the texel changed from boot to
boot; a hovered item reappeared because DWM redraws it in a batch whose t0 is
its own texture.  Writing ffffffff into the texel from the host brought the
text back without a redraw of the guest.

Fix: only an IMMUTABLE resource whose contents came with the create call is
GpuReadonly.  One created empty keeps its access trackers like a DEFAULT
resource, so the upload blit updates a fence and the first readers wait for
it.  A shm-shared texture is never GpuReadonly (an imported one is always
created DEFAULT, so it was tracked before too; the producer one is created
empty).  A
conforming D3D11 application always passes initial data for an IMMUTABLE
resource, so on Wine those keep the untracked path.

tests/native-d3d11-immutable-update.cpp reproduces the stale read on the host
alone (with the unpatched library every run reads stale data for tens to
hundreds of 32768 texture copies and buffer rounds) and passes with this patch.
--- a/src/d3d11/d3d11_buffer.cpp
+++ b/src/d3d11/d3d11_buffer.cpp
@@ -87,7 +87,13 @@
     if (!m_parent->IsTraced() && pDesc->Usage == D3D11_USAGE_DYNAMIC)
       flags.set(BufferAllocationFlag::CpuWriteCombined);
     // if (pDesc->Usage != D3D11_USAGE_DEFAULT)
-    if (pDesc->Usage == D3D11_USAGE_IMMUTABLE)
+    /* GpuReadonly is NoTracking: no encoder waits for a write to the
+     * allocation.  That holds only when the contents come with the create
+     * call.  An IMMUTABLE buffer created empty is filled afterwards by
+     * UpdateSubresource, a blit on the command stream (the native embedder
+     * delivers a guest's initial data this way), so its readers need the
+     * fences a DEFAULT buffer gets. */
+    if (pDesc->Usage == D3D11_USAGE_IMMUTABLE && pInitialData)
       flags.set(BufferAllocationFlag::GpuReadonly);
     if (pDesc->Usage != D3D11_USAGE_DYNAMIC) {
       if (pInitialData)
--- a/src/d3d11/d3d11_texture_device.cpp
+++ b/src/d3d11/d3d11_texture_device.cpp
@@ -514,7 +514,14 @@
   flags.set(finalDesc.CPUAccessFlags ? TextureAllocationFlag::GpuManaged : TextureAllocationFlag::GpuPrivate);
   if (!(finalDesc.BindFlags & (D3D11_BIND_UNORDERED_ACCESS | D3D11_BIND_RENDER_TARGET | D3D11_BIND_DEPTH_STENCIL)))
     flags.set(TextureAllocationFlag::ShaderReadonly);
-  if (finalDesc.Usage == D3D11_USAGE_IMMUTABLE)
+  /* GpuReadonly is NoTracking: no encoder waits for a write to the
+   * allocation.  That holds only when the contents come with the create call
+   * (the initializer queue, which every chunk waits for).  An IMMUTABLE texture
+   * created empty is filled afterwards by UpdateSubresource, a blit on the
+   * command stream (the native embedder delivers a guest's initial data this
+   * way), so the blits and draws reading it need the fences a DEFAULT texture
+   * gets; without them Metal may run a reader before the upload. */
+  if (finalDesc.Usage == D3D11_USAGE_IMMUTABLE && pInitialData)
     flags.set(TextureAllocationFlag::GpuReadonly);
   if (single_subresource && (finalDesc.BindFlags & D3D11_BIND_DEPTH_STENCIL)) {
     Rc<RenamableTexturePool> renamable = new RenamableTexturePool(texture.ptr(), 32, flags);
--- a/src/d3d11/d3d11_texture_shared_native.cpp
+++ b/src/d3d11/d3d11_texture_shared_native.cpp
@@ -184,7 +184,8 @@
 
   Flags<TextureAllocationFlag> flags;
   flags.set(TextureAllocationFlag::Shared);
-  if (finalDesc.Usage == D3D11_USAGE_IMMUTABLE)
-    flags.set(TextureAllocationFlag::GpuReadonly);
+  /* Never GpuReadonly: an imported texture is always DEFAULT
+   * (ImportSharedTexture), and the producer creates an IMMUTABLE one empty
+   * and uploads into it on the command stream, which has to be tracked. */
   if (!(finalDesc.BindFlags & (D3D11_BIND_UNORDERED_ACCESS | D3D11_BIND_RENDER_TARGET | D3D11_BIND_DEPTH_STENCIL)))
     flags.set(TextureAllocationFlag::ShaderReadonly);
